Overlay patch 0050 (D-062): NEVER START ON A RENDERER THE BUILD DOES NOT CONTAIN.

THE CRASH (OTA 1.1.1.1, the user's iPhone, 2026-10-09): SIGSEGV null deref in
Fast::Interpreter::Init <- Fast3dWindow::Init <- Context::InitWindow <- OTRGlobals
<- InitOTR <- SDL_main, every launch. The saved config had
Window.Backend.Id = 2 / Name = "OpenGL" (LUS enum: OPENGL=2, METAL=3).

THE LANDMINE (this LUS pin): the Fast3dWindow constructor calls
AddAvailableWindowBackend(FAST3D_SDL_OPENGL) unconditionally, but the iOS build
never defines ENABLE_OPENGL (libultraship/src/CMakeLists.txt's iOS branch; no
GfxRenderingAPIOGL symbol in the binary). So GetSavedWindowBackend() accepted
id 2 as "available", InitWindowManager's switch fell to `default:` (the OpenGL
case is #ifdef ENABLE_OPENGL), left mWindowManagerApi / mRenderingApi null, and
mInterpreter->Init(null, null, ...) crashed. The same bogus entry made the
Settings > "Renderer API (Needs reload)" combobox offer OpenGL on iOS:
picking it bricked the app with no in-app way back.

THE FIX (three parts):
  1. Constructor: AddAvailableWindowBackend(FAST3D_SDL_OPENGL) only
     #ifdef ENABLE_OPENGL -- exactly what newer LUS does (Ghostship/PaperBoat
     pins). A saved id 2 is then rejected, GetSavedWindowBackend returns the
     first available (Metal), SetWindowBackend rewrites the config: self-heal,
     no user action. The combobox lists Metal only.
  2. InitWindowManager `default:` on __APPLE__: SPDLOG_ERROR, then construct the
     Metal pair and record FAST3D_SDL_METAL instead of leaving nulls.
  3. Window::GetSavedWindowBackend: when the saved id is rejected, record
     "saved id X not available; using Y (unix T)" in the config key
     Window.Backend.LastRejected (saved with the config by SetWindowBackend) and
     SPDLOG_INFO it. The window is created in the OTRGlobals constructor BEFORE
     2S2H initialises its file logger (OTRGlobals::Initialize), so a log line
     from there only reaches stdout (found on the sim: absent from the log
     file). BenPort therefore re-logs the key as one SOH_BACKEND INFO line right
     after InitLogging, every launch while the key exists: a future log/config
     pull shows whether, and when, the value flipped again. The `default:`
     fallback of (2) records the same key.

